Z||000048||tomcat06085||11-16-2001||07:07 PM||||Hey Guys...be easy on that mobile. I'm 56 years old and watched the James Dean cruisers go out of style and the Peace & Love Beetles...VW, that is...come in and watched them go out of style. Everyone's got their own dream machine. I remember, very fondly I might add, my 1954 Baby Blue Ford station that had six speakers for an AM radio that probably didn't get more than 7 or 8 stations with the antenna alll the way up. It had 56 Cadilac hubcaps from Midnight Auto Supply and my father suggested " Play Pen " for a name painted on front quarter panel. It was pinstriped and nosed and decked...a sweet ride that all the girls wanted to enjoy at the drive-in movies. Guys...I'm almost in tears bringing back all those timeless memories. <P>I'M SURE I WOULD HAVE TRADED FOR THAT MUSTANG...yea...I'm real sure. We allhave a dream car...of course, all those dreams were changed when I got behind the wheel of a 1964 MG Midget...no radio, pure performance! The days, the days of old.||||reg||6
Z||000049||Back On Holiday||11-17-2001||01:06 AM||||Any two of them in that would cause the car to drag bottom like the Exxon Valdez on a sand bar.<P>Around here where I live, the Mushroom Capital of the world, Mexicans are very abundant also, their favorite cars are Trans Ams, Toyota Corollas, Honda civics, Dodge neons(the richer ones) and yes, Pontiac Fieros. - with all the Maint. they require, the Fiero doesn't usually last too long.  I just can't figure out what they do with them, cause they up and disappear one day...maybe that's where Fiero jon gets all his cars.   oh, they usually drive the 84's with the headlights up, I tried conversing with a guy who worked at burger king about his Red 84se, but he couldn't understand me very well, thought I wanted to buy it.<P><BR>||||reg||1
